Keep host-only cookies of hosts that are themselves public suffixes.

The public suffix list's private section names hosting providers' customer
domains (httpbin.org, postman-echo.com and many more). The curl cookie store
refused every cookie whose domain is a public suffix, so such a host could keep
no cookie at all, from a response or from script. Other browsers keep its
host-only cookies; so does this store now. A cookie for a suffix other than the
host itself is still refused.

diff --git a/Source/WebCore/platform/network/curl/CookieJarDB.cpp b/Source/WebCore/platform/network/curl/CookieJarDB.cpp
index dfd5de50..2fd40264 100644
--- a/Source/WebCore/platform/network/curl/CookieJarDB.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/platform/network/curl/CookieJarDB.cpp
@@ -500,7 +500,10 @@ static bool checkSecureCookie(const Cookie& cookie)
 
 bool CookieJarDB::canAcceptCookie(const Cookie& cookie, const URL& firstParty, const URL& url, CookieJarDB::Source source)
 {
-    if (PublicSuffixStore::singleton().isPublicSuffix(cookie.domain))
+    // A host that is itself on the public suffix list (as hosting providers
+    // list their customers' domains) still keeps its own host-only cookies, as
+    // other browsers do; only cookies for a suffix it does not equal are refused.
+    if (PublicSuffixStore::singleton().isPublicSuffix(cookie.domain) && cookie.domain != url.host().convertToASCIILowercase())
         return false;
 
     bool fromJavaScript = source == CookieJarDB::Source::Script;
